   "source": [
    "from PIL import Image\n",
    "image = Image.open(\"Ebbinghaus.jpg\")\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(9, 3))\n",
    "cmaps = [\"gray\", \"viridis\", \"jet\"]\n",
    "for i, cmap in enumerate(cmaps):\n",
    "    sns.heatmap(image, ax=ax[i], cmap=cmap)\n",
    "    ax[i].axis(\"equal\")\n",
    "    ax[i].axis(\"off\")\n",
    "    ax[i].set_title(cmap)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "19be0c10",
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "fragment"
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "The `viridis` color map looks similar to the original photo, but uses variation in color to make it easier to see highlights and shadows. The `jet` color map distorts the data, making the image appear to have color \"bands\" that are not in the actual photo. It also makes it hard to tell which parts of the image are supposed to be light, and which are supposed to be dark.\n",
    "\n",
    "To avoid these problems, use one of Seaborn's [perceptually uniform palettes](https://seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial/color_palettes.html#perceptually-uniform-palettes)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c8413418",
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "slide"
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "## Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "### Plot types\n",
    "\n",
    "Seaborn makes it relatively easy to make common types of plots.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Distributional* plots can be used to visualize variation in some measurement.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Categorical* plots can be used to visualize how a measurement differs between experimental conditions.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Relational* plots can be used to visualize relationships between variables."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f69d5e1c",
   "metadata": {
    "editable": true,
    "slideshow": {
     "slide_type": "fragment"
    },
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "### Color Palettes\n",
    "\n",
    "Color palettes built into Seaborn help to visualize variables.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Qualitative* palettes are designed for representing unordered categories.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Sequential* palettes are designed for representing values that vary over some range."
   ]
  }
